# Manuscript PDF page 46

                         HERALDS OF THE CEMÍ



  I looked at the street, at the third-floor window, now dark. At the
spot on the sidewalk where a woman I’d never met had stopped on her
way home. At my hand, where there was still a thin red line I hadn’t
wiped off.
  “Yeah,” I said.
  Manny froze with the pastelito halfway to his mouth.
  “Say that again. Slower. I’m recording.”



Mamá was in the kitchen when I got home, in the dark, at the table,
with the rosary wrapped around her fist. She didn’t turn on the light.
   “Doña Fela says the whole block felt something at the café,” she
said. “She says her sugar went down.”
   “Maybe it was the pastelitos.”
   “Maybe it was Manny.” She looked at me. Then at my lip, where a
thin dark line was still dried on it. She didn’t ask. She stood, wet a nap‐
kin at the sink, and wiped it off, the way she did when I was six and
came home with a scraped chin and told her I hadn’t fallen. “Siéntate.”
   She put a plate of rice and beans in front of me. I ate all of it. It was
nearly midnight and I hadn’t eaten since the pastelito, and it was the
best food I’ve ever had.
   “Gracias, Má.”
   “Mm.” She took the plate. “Tu abuela dice que te cuides.”
   “When did Abuela say that?”
   She didn’t answer. She rinsed the plate and went to bed.



I didn’t touch Chismosa that night.
   I lay on top of the covers in my room, with the blind half down and
the radiators knocking and the 4 going by every eight minutes like a
clock that had learned to sing, and my hand did what it’s done since I
was six. It found the headboard. It tapped a small rhythm on the wood,
over and over, soft, an old reflex I’d never once thought about.
   I turned it over now.



